  "title": "Bessemer process and cheap steel",
  "summary": "In August 1856 Henry Bessemer presented his method for making iron and steel without fuel to the British Association meeting at Cheltenham. Compressed air blown through molten iron burned out impurities such as excess carbon, making bulk steel possible. Early steel suffered from phosphorus in the ore, a problem later resolved by Sidney Gilchrist Thomas. The process proved massively significant for railways and large construction.",
